.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_TIMED_RECV_ZC 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc \- receive a message from a dynamic queue with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq,
                                       c_message_t *target_buf,
                                       uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function waits for a message in the dynamic queue for at most the
given timeout. If a message arrives in time, the function removes the
message at the head and transfers the ownership of its data pointer to
the caller in target_buf. The caller must free target_buf->data after
it finishes with the data.

The timeout is a count of microseconds from the call, from which the
library makes an absolute deadline on CLOCK_MONOTONIC.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq          Dynamic queue to receive from
target_buf  Buffer that gets the message together with the ownership
            of the data.
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds, from the call. 0
            does not wait, which makes the call ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success              - The function received a message. The caller
                            must free target_buf->data.
ccol_invalid_args         - dq or target_buf is NULL
ccol_container_empty      - The timeout is 0 and the queue is empty
ccol_timed_out            - The timeout ended before a message arrived
ccol_unexpected_failure   - The wait failed with an error other than a
                            timeout; errno holds that error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function blocks for the duration of the timeout at most.
On success, the caller gets the ownership of target_buf->data.
A timeout of 0 does not wait: the call then does exactly what
ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc does and returns its result, so it gives
ccol_container_empty, not ccol_timed_out, when it cannot proceed at
once.
A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, saturates
to the latest deadline that the clock can hold and in practice waits
with no end; it never wraps into the past.
The library measures the timeout against CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change
to the wall clock of the system (by an administrator, by NTP, or by the
resume of a virtual machine) does not make the timeout longer or
shorter: the call waits for the duration that the caller gave.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_send_zc (3)
